# Target C-II — canonical reflection blockers expose ordered antipodal source-hull caps

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only strengthening of the current shallow-tooth reflection branch; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** Type-II full-core shallow-tooth branch, specialized to the current `eta=3/26` slow-scale central braid  
**Dependencies:** reflection/perimeter collapse; source diameter bound; slow-scale central reflection braid  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_tooth_reflection_canonical_hull_blocker_2026_09_17.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The current reflection-braid theorems choose an arbitrary upper blocker for each retained shallow tooth. There is a stronger canonical choice that converts every blocker into an exact source-hull support normal.

For a reflected apex `x^-`, choose a source point `y` maximizing `|y-x^-|`. Since the reflection theorem already supplies some upper blocker at reflected distance greater than `D`, this canonical maximizer satisfies

\[
L:=|y-x^-|>D.
\]

It may be chosen as a vertex of the full source hull `K=conv(A)`. If

\[
N:={y-x^-\over L},
\]

then `N` is an exact outward support normal of `K` at `y`, and `y` is uniquely exposed by `N`.

For one shallow tooth write

\[
a=p-ct,\qquad b=p+ct,\qquad x=p+hn,\qquad x^-=p-hn,
\]

with

\[
|t|=|n|=1,\qquad t\perp n,
\qquad c=\cos\alpha,\qquad h=\sin\alpha,
\qquad 0<\alpha<\pi/6.
\]

Let `theta` be the acute angle between the canonical support normal `N` and the tooth inward normal `n`. The two loaded base endpoints alone force

\[
\boxed{
0\le D-W_K(N)
< L-W_K(N)
\le \sin(\alpha-\theta),
\qquad 0\le\theta<\alpha.
}
\tag{CH.1}
\]

Thus an upper reflection obstruction canonically produces a full-source width direction that is within a fixed subunit amount of the actual diameter.

The same calculation traps the entire tooth in the opposite support cap. Relative to the support line opposite `y`, the reflected point `x^-` lies strictly outside `K`, while

\[
\boxed{
0\le\operatorname{depth}_N(x)<2h
}
\tag{CH.2}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
0\le\operatorname{depth}_N(a),\operatorname{depth}_N(b)
<\sin(2\alpha).
}
\tag{CH.3}
\]

At the current slow-scale threshold

\[
\eta_*={3\over26},
\qquad
\cos\alpha_*={49\over52},
\qquad
\sin\alpha_*={\sqrt{303}\over52},
\]

these become

\[
\boxed{
D-W_K(N)<{\sqrt{303}\over52}=0.3347479843\ldots,
}
\tag{CH.4}
\]

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{depth}_N(x)<{\sqrt{303}\over26}
=0.6694959686\ldots<1,
}
\tag{CH.5}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{depth}_N(a),\operatorname{depth}_N(b)
<{49\sqrt{303}\over1352}
=0.6308712013\ldots<1.
}
\tag{CH.6}
\]

This canonical choice propagates through the current slow-scale central reflection braid without any density loss. For every

\[
\kappa_D\to\infty,
\qquad
\kappa_D=o(D^{1/4}),
\]

there is a family of more than

\[
\boxed{
(0.129220199818177\ldots-o(1))
{D^{1/4}\over\kappa_D}
}
\tag{CH.7}

pairwise vertex-disjoint, pairwise properly crossing reflection blockers such that:

- every blocker head is an exposed full-source hull vertex;
- every reflected blocker ray is the exact outward hull normal at that head;
- all mutual blocker crossings remain in the same `O(D/kappa_D)=o(D)` central core;
- the five-point tooth/reflection/penetration gadgets remain pairwise disjoint;
- every opposite tooth triple `{a_i,b_i,x_i}` lies in the subunit support cap given by (CH.5)--(CH.6).

The canonical hull normals also inherit the selected base-normal order. Their consecutive separation is

\[
\boxed{
(2-o(1))\kappa_DD^{-1/4},
}
\tag{CH.8}

and their total angular span is bounded below independently of `kappa_D`:

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{span}\{N_i\}
>
0.258440399636354\ldots-o(1)
\text{ radians},
}
\tag{CH.9}

about `14.8075` degrees. Because each `N_i` uniquely exposes its blocker head, the blocker heads occur on `partial K` in exactly the same cyclic order as the selected loaded-base normals.

The reflection survivor is therefore no longer an arbitrary family of `D-O(1)` inactive chords. It contains a near-quarter-power crossing braid of exact source-hull normal braces: one exposed hull vertex on one side, one disjoint shallow tooth trapped in a subunit antipodal cap on the other side, with both ends ordered over a fixed normal arc and all brace crossings confined to an `o(D)` central core.

---

## 1. Farthest reflected blockers are exposed hull vertices

Fix one shallow tooth and put

\[
q:=x^-=p-hn.
\]

The reflection/perimeter theorem supplies at least one source point `z` with

\[
|z-q|>D.
\tag{CH.10}
\]

Consider the convex function

\[
f(w)=|w-q|^2
\]

on the compact polygon

\[
K=\operatorname{conv}(A).
\]

A maximum of a convex function on a finite polygon is attained at a vertex. Every vertex of `K` is a source point. Choose a hull vertex `y` satisfying

\[
L:=|y-q|
=\max_{w\in K}|w-q|
=\max_{z\in A}|z-q|.
\tag{CH.11}
\]

Equation (CH.10) gives

\[
\boxed{L>D.}
\tag{CH.12}
\]

Put

\[
N={y-q\over L}.
\tag{CH.13}
\]

For any `z in K`, maximality gives `|z-q|<=L`. Expanding around `y`,

\[
|z-q|^2
=L^2+2L\,N\cdot(z-y)+|z-y|^2.
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{
N\cdot(z-y)
\le-{ |z-y|^2\over2L}
\le0.
}
\tag{CH.14}
\]

The inequality is strict for every `z!=y`. Thus the line through `y` perpendicular to `N` supports `K`, `N` is an outward support normal, and `y` is the unique point of `K` on that support line.

This is stronger than merely replacing an arbitrary blocker by a hull vertex: the reflected blocker ray itself is an exact exposing normal.

---

## 2. The reflected point lies beyond the opposite support line

Write

\[
H:=h_K(N)=N\cdot y,
\qquad
m:=\min_{z\in K}N\cdot z,
\qquad
W:=H-m=W_K(N).
\tag{CH.15}
\]

Since `diam(K)<=D`,

\[
W\le D.
\tag{CH.16}
\]

Because `q=y-LN`,

\[
N\cdot q=H-L.
\]

Using `L>D>=W`,

\[
H-L<H-W=m.
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{N\cdot x^-<m.}
\tag{CH.17}
\]

The reflected apex is strictly outside `K` beyond the support line opposite the exposed blocker head.

The base midpoint `p`, the loaded endpoints `a,b`, and the original apex `x` all belong to `K`: `a,b,x` are source points and `p=(a+b)/2`. Thus the short reflection segment from `x^-` toward the original tooth actually enters the opposite support cap of the full source hull.

---

## 3. Base feasibility forces the exact support-normal cone

Choose the sign of `t` so that

\[
N\cdot t\ge0.
\]

Write

\[
N\cdot n=\cos\theta,
\qquad
N\cdot t=\sin\theta.
\tag{CH.18}
\]

Define the opposite-cap depth of the base midpoint

\[
s_p:=N\cdot p-m.
\tag{CH.19}
\]

Since

\[
y=q+LN=p-hn+LN,
\]

we have

\[
H=N\cdot p-hN\cdot n+L,
\]

so

\[
\boxed{
s_p=W-L+h\cos\theta.
}
\tag{CH.20}
\]

The base endpoints have depths

\[
N\cdot a-m=s_p-c\sin\theta,
\qquad
N\cdot b-m=s_p+c\sin\theta.
\]

Both are nonnegative because `a,b in K`. Hence

\[
\boxed{s_p\ge c\sin\theta.}
\tag{CH.21}
\]

But `W<L`, so (CH.20) also gives

\[
s_p<h\cos\theta.
\tag{CH.22}
\]

In particular `cos(theta)>0`. Combining (CH.20)--(CH.22),

\[
0<L-W
\le h\cos\theta-c\sin\theta.
\tag{CH.23}
\]

Since `h=sin(alpha)` and `c=cos(alpha)`,

\[
h\cos\theta-c\sin\theta
=\sin(\alpha-\theta).
\tag{CH.24}
\]

The left side is positive, so

\[
\boxed{0\le\theta<\alpha.}
\tag{CH.25}
\]

Finally `L>D` implies

\[
0\le D-W<L-W.
\]

Equations (CH.23)--(CH.25) prove (CH.1).

This recovers the normal-alignment part of the reflection theorem from full-source support geometry alone, and also supplies the width-deficit term that the earlier local coordinate proof did not record.

---

## 4. The tooth occupies a subunit cap opposite the blocker head

The apex depth is

\[
\begin{aligned}
s_x
&:=N\cdot x-m\\
&=N\cdot(p+hn)-m\\
&=s_p+h\cos\theta.
\end{aligned}
\]

Using (CH.22),

\[
\boxed{
0\le s_x<2h\cos\theta\le2h.
}
\tag{CH.26}
\]

For the two base endpoints, the larger depth is

\[
s_p+c\sin\theta.
\]

Again by (CH.22),

\[
s_p+c\sin\theta
<h\cos\theta+c\sin\theta
=\sin(\alpha+\theta).
\]

Since `theta<alpha<pi/6`,

\[
\boxed{
\max\{\operatorname{depth}_N(a),
       \operatorname{depth}_N(b)\}
<\sin(2\alpha).
}
\tag{CH.27}
\]

The reflected point itself has signed depth

\[
N\cdot x^- -m
=H-L-m
=W-L<0.
\tag{CH.28}
\]

Thus the reflection crosses the actual opposite support line:

```text
x^- : strictly outside K beyond the opposite support line;
a,b  : inside a cap of depth < sin(2 alpha);
x    : inside a cap of depth < 2 sin(alpha).
```

No near-disk asymptotic is used here.

---

## 5. Exact specialization to `eta=3/26`

For an `eta`-shallow unit tooth,

\[
2c\ge2-\eta,
\qquad
c\ge1-{\eta\over2}.
\]

At

\[
\eta_*={3\over26},
\]

we have

\[
\boxed{
c\ge {49\over52},
\qquad
h\le {\sqrt{303}\over52},
\qquad
2h\le {\sqrt{303}\over26}.
}
\tag{CH.29}
\]

The current slow-scale braid uses exactly this threshold, and

\[
{303\over676}<1.
\]

From (CH.1),

\[
D-W_K(N)
<\sin(\alpha-\theta)
\le\sin\alpha=h
\le{\sqrt{303}\over52},
\]

which proves (CH.4).

Equation (CH.26) gives

\[
\operatorname{depth}_N(x)
<2h
\le{\sqrt{303}\over26}
=0.669495968674200\ldots<1,
\]

which is (CH.5).

For the loaded base endpoints,

\[
\sin(2\alpha)
=2hc
\]

is increasing over the current interval `0<alpha<=alpha_*<pi/6`, so

\[
\sin(2\alpha)
\le2{\sqrt{303}\over52}{49\over52}
={49\sqrt{303}\over1352}
=0.630871201250689\ldots<1.
\]

This proves (CH.6).

Thus the complete three-point tooth lies in a strict subunit cap opposite the canonical blocker head, and the associated width deficit is below `0.335`.

---

## 6. The canonical hull brace remains a valid fixed-slack blocker

The original and reflected apexes satisfy

\[
|x-x^-|=2h
\le C_*:={\sqrt{303}\over26}<1.
\]

Therefore

\[
|y-x|
\ge|y-x^-|-|x-x^-|
>L-C_*
>D-C_*.
\tag{CH.30}
\]

So the canonical farthest blocker lies in exactly the same fixed-slack graph used by the current slow-scale braid theorem.

Let

\[
d={y-x\over|y-x|}
\]

be the original blocker direction. The vectors `y-x` and `y-x^-` differ by `x^--x`, whose norm is at most `C_*`. Since both vector lengths exceed `D-C_*`, the standard normalized-vector estimate gives

\[
\left|d-N\right|
\le {2C_*\over D-C_*}.
\tag{CH.31}
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{
\angle(d,N)
\le2\arcsin\left({C_*\over D-C_*}\right)
=O(D^{-1}).
}
\tag{CH.32}
\]

This is negligible compared with the `kappa_DD^-1/4` normal spacing in the slow-scale braid.

---

## 7. Re-run the slow-scale central braid with canonical blockers

The slow-scale theorem first selects flat shallow teeth by their inward base normals, using bins of width

\[
w_D=\kappa_DD^{-1/4},
\qquad
\kappa_D\to\infty,
\qquad
\kappa_D=o(D^{1/4}).
\]

Only after the tooth selection does it choose one upper blocker per selected tooth. At that choice point choose the canonical farthest blocker from Section 1.

Every downstream hypothesis remains valid:

- (CH.30) gives the same `D-C_*` fixed-slack bound;
- the fixed-slack midpoint and star estimates apply unchanged;
- the blocker/base-normal error remains `O(D^-1/4)`;
- the selected normal spacing dominates that error by the factor `kappa_D`;
- shared blocker endpoints are still impossible;
- all supporting-line intersections remain in the common `O(D/kappa_D)` central disk;
- the five-point tooth/reflection/penetration gadgets remain pairwise disjoint.

Thus the slow-scale count and central-core conclusion are unchanged, proving (CH.7).

Let `n_i` be the selected inward base normals and `N_i` the canonical hull support normals. The current braid theorem gives

\[
\angle(d_i,n_i)=O(D^{-1/4}),
\]

and (CH.32) gives

\[
\angle(N_i,d_i)=O(D^{-1}).
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\angle(N_i,n_i)=O(D^{-1/4})=o(w_D).
}
\tag{CH.33}
\]

Consecutive selected base normals are separated by at least

\[
2w_D=2\kappa_DD^{-1/4}.
\]

Consequently the canonical support normals satisfy

\[
\boxed{
\angle(N_i,N_{i+1})
\ge(2-o(1))\kappa_DD^{-1/4},
}
\tag{CH.34}
\]

which is (CH.8).

---

## 8. The exposed blocker heads preserve the loaded-base cyclic order

All selected base normals lie in one quadrant. Equation (CH.33), together with the diverging spacing factor `kappa_D`, implies that the canonical support normals have exactly the same strict angular order for all sufficiently large `D`.

For a convex polygon, the interiors of its vertex normal cones occur in the same cyclic order as the vertices. Equation (CH.14) says `N_i` uniquely exposes `y_i`, so `N_i` belongs to the interior of the normal cone at `y_i`. Hence increasing support-normal angle is equivalent to moving through the `y_i` in their cyclic order along `partial K`.

Thus

\[
\boxed{
\text{the exposed blocker heads }y_i
\text{ have the same cyclic index order as the selected loaded-base normals.}
}
\tag{CH.35}

The slow-scale theorem gives

\[
M_D>
(\rho_*-o(1)){D^{1/4}\over\kappa_D},
\qquad
\rho_*=0.129220199818177\ldots .
\tag{CH.36}
\]

The selected base-normal span is therefore at least

\[
(M_D-1)2\kappa_DD^{-1/4}
>
2\rho_*-o(1).
\]

The first and last canonical support normals differ from the corresponding base normals by only `O(D^-1/4)=o(1)`, so

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{span}\{N_i\}
>
2\rho_*-o(1)
=
0.258440399636354\ldots-o(1)
\text{ radians}.
}
\tag{CH.37}

This is (CH.9). Numerically the limiting floor is

\[
14.8075441548\ldots\text{ degrees}.
\]

The support-head arc therefore does not collapse even though the individual normal spacing tends to zero.

---

## 9. Resulting canonical central-braid normal form

For any chosen slow scale `kappa_D`, the reflection survivor now contains

\[
(0.129220199818177\ldots-o(1)){D^{1/4}\over\kappa_D}
\]

pairwise-disjoint five-point gadgets with all of the following simultaneous structure:

1. the loaded bases are pairwise nonadjacent loaded-hull sides and have one strict cyclic normal order;
2. the inactive blocker chords have length `D-O(1)`, cross pairwise properly, and all mutual crossings lie in one `O(D/kappa_D)=o(D)` central core;
3. each blocker head is an exposed vertex of the full source hull;
4. the reflected blocker ray is the exact outward support normal at that head;
5. the exposed blocker heads occur in the same cyclic order as the loaded-base normals;
6. the canonical support-normal family spans at least `0.25844-o(1)` radians;
7. every canonical width satisfies
   \[
   D-W_K(N_i)<0.334748;
   \]
8. each loaded base lies in a `<0.630872`-deep cap at the support line opposite its blocker head;
9. each shallow apex lies in the same opposite cap at depth `<0.669496`, while its reflected competitor lies strictly outside that support line;
10. the original blocker direction differs from the exact hull support normal by only `O(D^-1)`.

This removes the arbitrary blocker-head placement from the central reflection braid. The live escape is now a source-hull object: an ordered fixed-angle arc of exposed hull normals, each paired with a disjoint shallow tooth in a strict subunit antipodal cap, while the resulting almost-diametral braces form a near-quarter-power crossing braid through one sublinear central core.

The next closure target is correspondingly narrower. A useful theorem must couple the ordered antipodal caps, not merely the blocker chords. Two plausible routes are now concrete:

- prove that the required disjoint two-edge tooth/penetration gadgets cannot occupy all of these subunit opposite caps while their exposing normals sweep a fixed `>0.2584` radian arc; or
- use the exact support-normal rays to combine several discrete apex reflections with compensating inward motions at the exposed blocker heads, while preserving the loaded contacts and decreasing the active diameter system.

**Disposition:** replaced arbitrary slow-scale reflection blockers by canonical farthest hull blockers, proved an exact width-deficit law and strict subunit antipodal-cap localization, and propagated the canonical choice through the full `D^(1/4-o(1))` central crossing braid without density loss. C-II and Level I remain open.
